<h1 id="title">Animal Origin</h1>
<p class="subtitle" id="subtitle">Some ingredients are always animal. Others never say.</p>
<p class="description" id="description">BOTIA flags ingredients that are always animal-derived, and ingredients whose origin the label leaves undeclared.</p>
<div id="header_trigger_container"></div>
</section>
<section class="section">
<h2 class="section-title" id="why_title">Why this matters</h2>
<p class="section-intro" id="why_body">Gelatine, carmine, shellac and beeswax are always animal-derived. But many others &#8212; emulsifiers, enzymes, glycerol, flavourings &#8212; can be animal, plant or synthetic, and the label reads exactly the same either way.</p>
</section>
<section class="section">
<h2 class="section-title" id="what_title">What BOTIA looks for</h2>
<p class="section-intro" id="what_body">BOTIA separates two cases. Ingredients that are animal-derived by definition, and ingredients whose origin is simply not declared. The second group is not a verdict: it is the information the label did not give you.</p>
</section>
<section class="section">
<div class="can-cannot">
<div class="can-box">
<h4 id="can_title">BOTIA CAN</h4>
<ul>
<li id="can_1">Identify ingredients that are always animal-derived.</li>
<li id="can_2">Flag ingredients whose origin the label leaves undeclared.</li>
<li id="can_3">Explain why that distinction matters for the choice you are making.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="cannot-box">
<h4 id="cannot_title">BOTIA CANNOT</h4>
<ul>
<li id="cannot_1">Determine the species an ingredient came from.</li>
<li id="cannot_2">Confirm origin when the label does not declare it.</li>
<li id="cannot_3">Certify a product as vegetarian or vegan.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="closing">
<p class="closing-claim" id="closing">Undeclared is not forbidden. It is unknown &#8212; and that is worth knowing.</p>
